        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Skruuvi',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Skruuvi is a Finnish trick-taking card game for four players, a descendant of the Russian game Vint and a distant cousin of Bridge. It features bidding, misère contracts, and card exchange between partners.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Finland (game developed and played in Finland); ancestral game Vint from Russia.\nTime Period: Late 19th century (Vint introduced to Finland); Skruuvi codified by 1940s.\nHistorical Development: Descendant of the Russian game Vint; when Vint was introduced to Finland in the late 19th century it was known as Skruv-Whist (Swedish) and Skruuvi (Finnish); rules diverged and were codified in the 1940s.\nCultural Significance: Played in Finland as a niche hobby; Helsingin Suomalainen Klubi organizes annual Toro Skruuvi tournament.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Varies by player count\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise\n--',
              },
            ],
          },
